  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Given that $h(t)=5\\cdot {4}^{3t+6},$ find $t$ such that $h(t)=320.$\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we substitute $h(t)=320$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot {4}^{3t+6}=320\n$$\n\nWe can divide both sides of the equation by the constant factor of $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\cdot {4}^{3t+6}}{5} & =\\frac{320}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}\\cdot {4}^{3t+6}}{\\cancel{5}} & =64 \\\\ {4}^{3t+6} & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $t$ using the fact that $64$ is a power of $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{3t+6} & ={4}^{3} \\\\ 3t+6 & =3 \\\\ 3t & =-3 \\\\ t & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
